{
  "id": "rec-025",
  "pattern": "recursion-termination",
  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "The base case returns 1 for both n === 0 and n === 1, but the correct base case for n === 0 is 0 — fib(0) should be 0, not 1. Because every later value is built from the two before it, this single wrong base case shifts the entire sequence: fibSeries(6) returns [1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8] instead of [0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5]. Each per-call `memo = {}` default means memoization never carries over between top-level calls in the loop, but that's not the bug — the wrong base value would produce the same shifted sequence even without memoization at all.",
  "prompt": "fib is meant to compute the standard Fibonacci sequence (0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, ...). Step through fibSeries(6) and predict what it returns.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function fib(n, memo = {}) {\n  if (n in memo) return memo[n];\n  if (n <= 1) return 1;\n  const result = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n  memo[n] = result;\n  return result;\n}\nfunction fibSeries(count) {\n  const seq = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n    seq.push(fib(i));\n  }\n  return seq;\n}\nconsole.log(fibSeries(6));",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
